---
/**
 * /learn/mev-protected-swaps: evergreen canonical answer page for MEV
 * protection. States precisely what IS protected and keeps the qualified
 * "not an absolute guarantee" wording next to every strong claim (same
 * sentence as the homepage FAQ; do not strengthen it here).
 */
import ContentPage from '../../layouts/ContentPage.astro'
import { canonicalPage } from '../../data/canonicalPages'

const { updated } = canonicalPage('/learn/mev-protected-swaps/')

const FAQS = [
  {
    q: 'What is MEV?',
    a: `MEV (maximal extractable value) is profit extracted by reordering, inserting, or censoring transactions around yours. For traders the common forms are front-running, where someone executes ahead of your visible pending trade, and sandwiching, where an attacker buys before your trade moves the price and sells right after it, pocketing the difference your slippage tolerance allowed.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How does a sandwich attack work?',
    a: `Your swap sits in the public mempool with a slippage tolerance. A searcher spots it, places a buy just before it to push the price up, lets your trade execute at the worse price you tolerated, then sells immediately after. Your slippage setting is exactly the budget the attacker harvests. The attack requires seeing your trade before it executes and being able to order around it.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do batch auctions mitigate MEV?',
    a: `Orders are submitted offchain, trades of a pair clear at a uniform batch price, and solvers compete on execution quality. These reduce common ordering advantages. Settlement transactions using external liquidity can still be public, and price improvement is shared under the applicable fee policies.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the signed limit price guarantee?',
    a: `An order can never be settled below its signed limit price. That bound is enforced by the settlement contract, not by the goodwill of any solver: a settlement that violates it reverts. On the standard-policy chains, improvement over the reference quote is shared under the capped pricing policy. Arc uses the same capped backend improvement policy.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is MEV protection guaranteed?',
    a: `Orders settle through CoW Protocol batch auctions, which are designed to protect trades from MEV such as front-running and sandwiching by settling at a uniform clearing price. An order can never be settled below its signed limit price. Batch-auction settlement mitigates MEV by design; it is not an absolute guarantee against all adversarial conditions.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I pay gas for an MEV-protected swap?',
    a: `Not for ERC-20 orders: the winning solver submits the settlement transaction and pays the gas, while the applicable chain-aware trading charge is taken from the trade. Selling native ETH is the exception: it places an on-chain EthFlow transaction that pays normal network gas, and on chains where the EthFlow contract is not deployed you wrap ETH to WETH first, which also costs gas. Token approvals you sign separately cost gas too.`,
  },
]
---

<ContentPage
  title="MEV-protected swaps, explained | Ophis"
  description="What MEV and sandwich attacks are, how batch-auction settlement mitigates them, what a signed limit price actually guarantees, and what is not guaranteed."
  path="/learn/mev-protected-swaps/"
  heading="MEV-protected swaps, explained"
  lede="What sandwich attacks actually exploit, which of those levers batch auctions remove, and the precise boundary of the protection."
  updated={updated}
  faqs={FAQS}
>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> offchain orders and uniform clearing prices per token
    pair reduce common MEV opportunities. Your signed limit is enforced by the settlement
    contract, but the settlement transaction can still be public and use external pools.
    This is mechanism-level mitigation, not an absolute guarantee against every attack.
  </p>

  {FAQS.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{f.q}</h2>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    The mechanism behind this page is covered in
    <a href="/learn/intent-based-dex-aggregator/">what is an intent-based DEX aggregator</a>. Fees
    per path and per chain are on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>, and which chains settle
    on Ophis-operated contracts is on <a href="/supported-chains/">supported chains</a>. For a
    narrative walkthrough with examples, see the blog post
    <a href="/blog/mev-protection-batch-auctions/">MEV protection and batch auctions</a>.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
